-- 0.16, W331 (R75): the two credential writes that take the fence exclusive wait for each lock `LOCK_WAIT_MS` at most (`@coffre/core/vault`, 500 ms, half a use's own), as every other wait but a use's: a writer waiting for the fence holds back every use asking after it in Postgres's queue, so it leaves the queue within half a use's wait, and its caller hears busy at once (`credentials.ts`, `BUSY_CODES`). They waited 1 s, a use's whole wait (0047; 0076 made `change_bindings` again, with it, so this comes after). `sign_in` and `issue_token` keep their 1 s: they take the issue lock shared, which no use asks for, and CI sign-in is a hot path of its own (R44). Their bodies are unchanged; `migrate-locks`'s test holds the setting to `LOCK_WAIT_MS`. Additive: the previous release's vault calls them as before, and hears busy sooner.
ALTER FUNCTION public.revoke_credential(text,bigint,text,text,bytea,bytea,bytea,uuid,bytea,bytea,bigint,text,boolean,bigint,uuid[],text[]) SET lock_timeout = '500ms';--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER FUNCTION public.change_bindings(bigint,text,boolean,text,integer,bigint,integer,bigint,uuid,bytea,bigint,uuid,integer,bigint,uuid[],text[],text,integer,uuid[],uuid[],text[],uuid,bytea,text,text,text,text,text,text,text,text,text,text,bigint,bytea,uuid[],bytea[],bytea[],bytea[],bytea[],uuid[],uuid[],bytea[],bytea[],bytea[],bytea[],bigint,text,text[],uuid[],text[],bytea[],bytea[]) SET lock_timeout = '500ms';
